WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.181 --> 00:00:01.830
Joe Rogan: Yeah, that's
what I'm talking about!

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:02.663
Look at him!

00:00:02.663 --> 00:00:04.320
[crunching]

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.070
Narrator: That sound
isn't really the sound

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.013
of a cockroach being chewed.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.040
It's actually something
much more familiar:

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.600
the sound of crunching M&amp;M's.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.110
[crunching]

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.770
You'd think the sounds in a reality show

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:23.310
like "Fear Factor" would be, well, real,

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.050
but they're created by Foley artists

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:28.090
like Monique Reymond.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.310
Monique has worked on her fair share

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.240
of reality competition shows,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.980
each of which has its own array of sounds,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.919
ranging from outrageous...

00:00:36.919 --> 00:00:40.344
[punching]
[water splashing]

00:00:40.344 --> 00:00:41.268
to subtle.

00:00:41.268 --> 00:00:43.870
[clinking]

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:47.580
But the real challenge is
creating the perfect balance

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.650
between realism and exaggeration.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.810
Monique Reymond: How did that sound?

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:54.350
Narrator: Reality game shows

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.280
use Foley artists to
create their sound effects

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.660
because it is often too complicated

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:03.530
to record high-quality sound
while filming those shows.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.360
And by using Foley,

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.550
these artists can add
dramatics to the show.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.380
There are some instances,
like with "Wipeout,"

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.002
when exaggeration is
actually the whole point.

00:01:15.002 --> 00:01:16.230
[crashing]

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:17.972
Monique: We would, for each show,

00:01:17.972 --> 00:01:21.495
do every punching glove and every impact.

00:01:21.495 --> 00:01:22.936
And the thing is, something like that,

00:01:22.936 --> 00:01:24.420
with production sound,

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:25.920
there's probably no impact at all.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.080
The glove just probably comes out,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.500
and the person falls off into the water.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.110
In order to make that

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.800
a more entertaining journey

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.550
for people to experience visually,

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:36.760
we would add a little bit of smack

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.210
to make it seem more
funny and more dramatic

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.860
and more interesting for people.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.743
Narrator: To create that extra smack,

00:01:43.743 --> 00:01:44.576
[smacking]

00:01:44.576 --> 00:01:48.590
Monique adds leafy greens
on top of the foam mat,

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.653
creating an extra little
crunch to each hit.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.450
However, some reality
game shows require Foley

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.392
that is a bit more realistic,

00:01:58.392 --> 00:02:00.558
like "American Gladiators."

00:02:00.558 --> 00:02:02.058
The foam-mat hits

00:02:02.058 --> 00:02:04.983
aren't embellished with any extra crunch,

00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:07.120
and the clinking of metal harnesses

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.528
adds some reality.

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:09.840
[clinking]

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.950
But in shows like "Fear Factor,"

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.710
the fine balance between
reality and dramatization

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.290
really comes into play.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:18.470
Monique: It's almost as
if, like, when you're in

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:19.745
a situation where you're nervous

00:02:19.745 --> 00:02:21.620
and you can almost hear your heartbeat,

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:22.830
you don't really hear your heartbeat,

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:23.980
but you feel it, right?

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.960
Well, so that's what we
try to do with the Foley

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.180
with these kinds of shows.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.703
Narrator: Creepy bugs are a
mainstay for "Fear Factor,"

00:02:30.703 --> 00:02:31.714
[contestant yells]

00:02:31.714 --> 00:02:33.780
so Monique had to figure out

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.590
how to create an array
of squeamish bug noises.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.710
Unlike crunchy cockroaches,

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.110
a bowl full of worms is created

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.201
by using an old car shammy.

00:02:44.201 --> 00:02:47.410
Monique: So, we soak
them in a tub of water.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.310
This is where the magic happens,

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.160
because they get really soft.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.430
And once these guys get soft,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.612
they can take on a life

00:02:55.612 --> 00:02:58.810
of so many different handy things.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:02.770
And this is gonna be perfect for worms.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.345
So, for when we do the
worms just kind of gushing,

00:03:05.345 --> 00:03:07.550
we're gonna just add a
little bit of moisture,

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.113
make them sound even grosser.

00:03:10.113 --> 00:03:12.260
[squishing]
Oh, yeah, that's nice.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.490
Narrator: And even the same type of bug

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:17.190
can make different sounds

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.000
depending on the situation.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.180
Creating Foley for a
tub full of cockroaches

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.480
is completely different
from crunching a cockroach.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.560
Monique: Sometimes you
nail sounds immediately.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.620
Other times you just have to work with it.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:34.710
And with the aquarium full of roaches,

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:39.390
the roach has kind of a
firm exoskeletal situation.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.030
Like, what would sound like those shells?

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.080
What we came up with rather quickly

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.822
was pistachios,

00:03:45.822 --> 00:03:47.400
but then that didn't really work.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.042
The pistachios sounded too small

00:03:50.042 --> 00:03:51.970
and almost too even.

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:54.880
And so then we added Brazil nuts,

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.290
then we added peanuts,

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.600
and that wasn't even quite it.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:03:59.783
And then you think about it,

00:03:59.783 --> 00:04:02.633
and what is the most creepy part

00:04:02.633 --> 00:04:04.680
of being surrounded with bugs

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.580
is having them crawl on you.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.610
So we were missing the
creepiness of the crawling.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:13.450
And so then we experimented
on a little pine cone

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.810
so that we could get that feeling

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:16.210
of being crawled upon,

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:18.170
like these things are under your clothes.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:19.690
Narrator: While Monique had figured out

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.653
the noises for the cockroaches,

00:04:21.653 --> 00:04:24.680
something still didn't
sound totally accurate

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.960
when the cockroaches were initially poured

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.860
into the tub with a contestant.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.680
So she had to adapt her Foley once again

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.460
to make sure that it sounded just right.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:35.710
Monique: Since there's somebody in there,

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:38.860
if I just were to pour
this into the plastic tub,

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.600
it wouldn't sound like
there was a body in there.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.060
So I added some gloves in here.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.160
If that's not quite enough,

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.266
I might stick a leather jacket in, too.

00:04:46.266 --> 00:04:48.291
[nuts clattering]

00:04:48.291 --> 00:04:50.810
Narrator: Monique says
that creating the realistic

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.440
balance of these sounds for "Fear Factor"

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.160
was especially difficult

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.490
because sometimes she had no reference

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.200
for what a noise sounded like.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.040
Monique: There was a specific
episode of "Fear Factor"

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.260
where somebody had to eat sheep eyes

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.410
versus Buffalo testicles.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.690
I don't know what either
sound would be, fortunately,

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.520
but, I don't know, if we were just to bite

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.310
into something that was soft and gushy,

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.822
that would probably be
what it sounded like.

00:05:17.822 --> 00:05:19.710
But to exaggerate it,

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.690
to make it a little bit more horrific,

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:24.760
we decided to use cherry tomatoes

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.550
because there was that little popping.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.210
There was that skin on the outside,

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:31.010
and there's that little
explosion of the tomato.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:32.960
We wanted this to sound

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.420
a little bit more upsetting.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:38.380
And Foley grounds this
in reality for people,

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:40.715
because if it was all music and score,

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:42.530
that might drive

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.440
the drama of the situation,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.200
the suspense of the situation,

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:47.460
but it's not gonna creep you out.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:48.660
Narrator: Now, game shows

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.170
aren't the only thing she's worked on,

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:54.410
but Monique has kind of
developed a reputation

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:57.520
as one of Hollywood's go-to Foley artists

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.300
for reality competitions.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.780
Monique: I worked on "Mad
Men" for a few seasons.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.040
What a beautifully written,
acted, stylized show.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:07.481
Now, for Foley,

00:06:07.481 --> 00:06:08.706
briefcase,

00:06:08.706 --> 00:06:10.110
hats,

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:11.410
glass of scotch,

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:12.470
tinkle some ice.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:13.920
All fine and well,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.300
but there is no opportunity

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.370
for imagining what a
maggot would sound like.

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:21.840
I have found creating Foley

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.350
for game shows to be actually

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.403
much more satisfying than
I would have imagined.

